---
// src/pages/projects/example-project.astro
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout title="Catan Collector — Nicholas Grzelak">
  <article class="project-page">
    <header class="project-header">
      <p class="label">PB-002 · Personal Build</p>
      <h1>Catan Collector</h1>
      <p class="project-lede">
        After a seeing dice roll after dice roll land on 5 one night (and not my 8s) I was curious
         to see if a single game was a large enough sample size to have normally distributed dice rolls. 
         Once the idea of tracking dice was spawned other things could be tracked like wins, and turn times.
      </p>
      <p class="label project-facts">
        2026 · Arduino · Circuit Design · Inventor · FDM printing 
      </p>
    </header>

    <!--
      Images: drop files into the /public folder of your project, e.g.
      public/projects/plotter/render-01.jpg, then reference them from the
      site root like below. Delete this placeholder block once you have
      real images.
    -->
    <figure class="project-figure">
      <!-- <div class="img-placeholder" aria-hidden="true">
        RENDER — ISO VIEW (replace with your image)
      </div> -->
        <img src="/projects/catancollector/IMG_6175.jpg" alt="Catan Collector Full View" />
      <!-- <img src="/projects/plotter/render-01.jpg" alt="Isometric CAD render of the plotter frame" /> -->
      <figcaption class="label">FIG. 1 — Catan Collector Full View</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>Overview</h2>
    <p>
      Turns out most games are normally distributed. Sometimes some rolls are represented more than others
       but dont bank on 4s. Thats not a real strategy. Like in finance diversity of rolls plays a huge part.
       Another side effect is that when everyone has a timer going with their name beside it, things get fast. 
       Like really fast. It does make the games that much more exciting.
    </p>

    <h2>Design</h2>
    <p>
      Initially the project was going to have a much smaller screen with a hat that plugged into the Arduino MEGA and use a Ardunio touchpad to input numbers.
      I liked the aesthetic as it gave it a mini laptop like shape with controls on the bottom and a screen on top.
      For this project I tested a variety of print in place hinges, I found that printing the pin with one piece and having the other piece with a clearance hole
      had the best tolerance, leading to the smoothest movement and least wobble. FIG. 2 below shows the original prototype with the connected touchpad. Ultimately the touch pad and bottom half
      were never used as my roommate lent me a whooping 5 in touchscreen for this project.
    </p>

    <figure class="project-figure">
      <img src="/projects/catancollector/IMG_5326.jpg" alt="Catan Collector Screen Picture" />
      <figcaption class="label">FIG. 2 — Assembled machine, first plot</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      The Catan Counter has an initialization screen to select players in the order that game will be played.
      Once more than 3 players are selected a game can commence by hitting the start. Then once the first players 
      rolls the dice and enters it by hitting the number on the graph, a counter will begin for that players turn. 
      The players turn is considered done when the next person enters their dice roll.
    </p>

     <figure class="project-figure">
      <img src="/projects/catancollector/IMG_5192.jpg" alt="Catan Collector Results" />
      <figcaption class="label">FIG. 3 — Catan Collector Results</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>Results</h2>
    <p>
      Unfortunately game stats could not be saved as the touch screen blocks the sd card slot. 
      Aggregate stats could not be kept but it was still fun to keep track of stats in a single game.
      The touch screen also would not accurately map where the screen was pressed so sometimes a few presses were needed.
      If I did this project again I would a much smaller microcontroller, something like the rp2040, this would allow for a smaller footprint,
      quick response times and an ease of programming.
    </p>

    <p class="back-link"><a href="/">← Back to index</a></p>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  .project-page {
    padding: 4rem 0 1rem;
  }

  .project-header {
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
  }

  .project-header h1 {
    font-size: clamp(1.7rem, 4vw, 2.4rem);
    margin-top: 1rem;
  }

  .project-lede {
    margin-top: 1.25rem;
    max-width: 52ch;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .project-facts {
    margin-top: 1.25rem;
  }

  .project-page h2 {
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    font-weight: 400;
    margin: 2.5rem 0 0.75rem;
  }

  .project-figure {
    margin: 2.5rem 0;
  }

  .project-figure figcaption {
    margin-top: 0.6rem;
  }

  .img-placeholder {
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule-strong);
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    text-align: center;
    padding: 1rem;
  }

  .back-link {
    margin-top: 3.5rem;
  }
</style>